window.docContents[9].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-every-copy-is-induced":{"contents":"A canonical subcube copy preserves Hamming distances: translation is an automorphism,\nand extendEmb d preserves the Hamming weight since the coordinates outside the\nimage of d are all false. So a canonical copy preserves graph distances, i.e. it\nis an isometric embedding.\n\nlemma hammingNorm_extendEmb {n k : ℕ} (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n) (z : Fin k → Bool) :\n    hammingNorm (extendEmb d z) = hammingNorm z := by\n  simp only [hammingNorm]\n  rw [← Finset.card_map d]\n  congr 1\n  ext j\n  by_cases hj : ∃ i, d i = j\n  · obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hj\n    simp\n  · simp_all [extendEmb_apply_of_not_exists _ hj, Bool.zero_eq_false]\n\n\nlemma hammingDist_extendEmb {n k : ℕ} (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n) (x y : Fin k → Bool) :\n    hammingDist (extendEmb d x) (extendEmb d y) = hammingDist x y := by\n  rw [hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm, hammingDist_eq_hammingNorm,\n    boolVec_neg, boolVec_neg, ← extendEmb_add, hammingNorm_extendEmb]\n\n\nlemma subCubeCopy_edist {n k : ℕ} (b : Fin n → Bool) (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n)\n    (x y : Fin k → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).edist (subCubeCopy b d x) (subCubeCopy b d y) =\n      (hypercubeHamming k).edist x y := by\n  have h := hammingDist_aut (hammingTranslate b) (extendEmb d x) (extendEmb d y)\n  simp only [hammingTranslate_apply] at h\n  rw [hypercubeHamming_edist_eq, hypercubeHamming_edist_eq, subCubeCopy_apply,\n    subCubeCopy_apply, h, hammingDist_extendEmb]\n\n\nSince adjacency is distance one, a canonical copy reflects adjacency as well as\npreserving it, and by rigidity every copy is canonical. So every copy of Q[k] in\nQ[n] is induced, and, as for the 5-cycles of Induced 5 Cycles,\nthe labelled count also counts the embeddings. We state both facts in the Hamming\nmodel, i.e. for hypercubeHamming k in hypercubeHamming n.\n\nlemma copy_hypercubeHamming_adj_iff {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) {x y : Fin k → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f x) (f y) ↔ (hypercubeHamming k).Adj x y := by\n  obtain ⟨b, d, rfl⟩ := copy_eq_subCubeCopy f\n  rw [← edist_eq_one_iff_adj, ← edist_eq_one_iff_adj, subCubeCopy_edist]\n\n\ntheorem card_embedding_hypercubeHamming (n k : ℕ) :\n    Nat.card (hypercubeHamming k ↪g hypercubeHamming n) =\n      2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k := by\n  rw [← Nat.card_congr (copyEquivEmbedding fun f _ _ =>\n      (copy_hypercubeHamming_adj_iff f).1), ← Fintype.card_eq_nat_card]\n  exact labelledCopyCount_hypercubeHamming n k\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Counting Copies of Subcubes*","header":"12.8.6. Every Copy is Induced","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-every-copy-is-induced"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-11-distance-results":{"contents":"* Iso.edist_eq: graph isomorphisms preserve the graph distance.* edist_eq_hammingDist: the graph distance between any two vertices in Q[n] is\n  exactly equal to their Hamming distance.* hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one: adjacency in Q[n] is equivalent to\n  Hamming distance one.* IsAcyclic.edist_eq_length: in an acyclic graph the distance between the endpoints\n  of a path is its length.* edist_pathNat₁ / ediam_pathNat₁ / diam_pathNat₁: in the infinite path the\n  distance from m to m + k is k, so its diameter is ⊤ and its ℕ-valued\n  diameter is the junk value 0.* Iso.eccent_eq / Iso.ediam_eq / Iso.radius_eq: eccentricity, diameter and\n  radius are isomorphism invariants.* IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq / IsVertexTransitive.radius_eq_ediam /\n  IsVertexTransitive.center_eq_univ: in a vertex-transitive graph all\n  eccentricities are equal, the radius equals the diameter and every vertex is\n  central.* ediam_boxProd: the diameter of a box product of graphs on nonempty vertex types\n  is the sum of the diameters.* ediam_hypercube / radius_hypercube: the hypercube Q[n] has diameter and\n  radius n.* mycielskian_radius_le_two / mycielskian_ediam_le_four: the Mycielskian of a\n  graph without isolated vertices has radius at most 2 and diameter at most 4.* mycielskian_diam_le_four: the ℕ-valued diameter of any Mycielskian is at most\n  4.* le_egirth_of_free: a graph containing no copy of C[n] for 3 ≤ n ≤ k has\n  girth at least k + 1.* cliqueFree_three_iff_free_cycleGraph: a graph is triangle-free iff it contains no\n  copy of C[3].* four_le_egirth_of_cliqueFree_three / five_le_egirth: a triangle-free graph has\n  girth at least 4, and at least 5 if it also has no 4-cycle.* egirth_cycleGraph / ediam_cycleGraph: the cycle graph C[n + 3] has girth\n  n + 3 and diameter (n + 3) / 2.* egirth_completeBipartiteGraph: the complete bipartite graph K[s + 2, t + 2] has\n  girth 4.* egirth_hypercube: the girth of the hypercube Q[n + 2] is 4.* egirth_mycielskian_eq_four / egirth_mycielskian_eq_five: for triangle-free G\n  the girth of mμ[G] is 4 if two edges of G share an endpoint, and 5 if G\n  is a matching plus isolated vertices with at least one edge.* egirth_le_edist_add_edist_add_two: two distinct neighbours a and b of v,\n  neither further from r than v, give a cycle of length at most\n  G.edist r a + G.edist r b + 2.* moore_bound / moore_bound_of_radius_le: the Moore bound on the order of a graph\n  of maximum degree at most d + 1 and diameter (or radius) at most k.* cage_bound_odd / cage_bound_even: the cage bounds on the order of a graph of\n  minimum degree at least δ + 1 and girth at least 2 * k + 1 (resp. 2 * k).* ediam_petersenGraph / egirth_petersenGraph: the Petersen graph has diameter 2\n  and girth 5, so it attains the Moore bound.* Colorable.extend_precoloring: Albertson's theorem, a precolouring of vertices\n  pairwise at distance at least 4 in a k-colourable graph extends to a\n  (k + 1)-colouring.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Results","header":"14.3.9. Chapter 11: Distance (Results)","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-11-distance-results"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-4-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees":{"contents":"* natNeDec / natEvenDec: Decidable instances for a ≠ b and Even m on ℕ,\n  synthesized by inferInstance, used to inspect which instance Lean picks.* myIfThenElse: branches with bif on the Boolean decide p from a Decidable p\n  instance to return a string, mirroring how ite is defined.* myClassicalIfThenElse: the same function built with classical, hence\n  noncomputable.* pathNat₂': pathNat₂ restated as an abbrev, so that Lean can synthesize\n  DecidableRel for its adjacency.* pathGraphAdjDecidableRw: deliberately flawed rw-based decidability instance,\n  illustrating how a non-definitional cast blocks kernel reduction and hence\n  decide.* locallyFiniteOfFintypeEdgeSet: a graph with finitely many edges is locally\n  finite.* degIn: the number of neighbours of a lying in a finite set s.* hypercubeVertToBin: conversion of a hypercube vertex to its binary string\n  representation.* degreeMultiset: the multiset of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph.* degreeSequence: the list of degrees of all vertices in a finite graph, sorted in\n  non-increasing order.* Graphic: predicate for a multiset being the degree multiset of some finite simple\n  graph.* erdosGallaiLE: the k-th Erdős–Gallai inequality for a multiset of degrees.* havelHakimiAux: the Havel–Hakimi test on a non-increasing list, merging rather\n  than re-sorting between rounds.* havelHakimi: decides whether a multiset of degrees is graphic via Havel–Hakimi.* coneGraph: adjoins a new vertex to a graph, adjacent to a chosen set of vertices.* havelHakimiEdges: constructive Havel–Hakimi, returning the edge list of a graph\n  realizing a labelled graphic degree sequence.* graphOfEdgeList: the graph on Fin n with a given list of edges, used to check\n  the output of havelHakimiEdges.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* locallyFiniteBlowUpGraph: a blow-up of a locally finite graph with finite classes\n  is locally finite.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.3. Chapter 4: Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-4-decidability-finiteness-and-degrees"},"/Distance/#section-counting-between-layers":{"contents":"Both bounds compare consecutive layers by counting the edges between them, using two\ndouble-counting lemmas about finsets A and B of vertices. Here\nG.degIn B u = #{v ∈ B | G.Adj u v} is the number of neighbours of u in B, as in\nNeighbours In A Set.\n\nWe count the adjacent pairs (u, v) with u ∈ A and v ∈ B.\n\n* If every vertex of B has a neighbour in A, and every vertex of A has at most\n  d neighbours in B, then #B ≤ d * #A, since each v ∈ B lies in at least one\n  such pair and each u ∈ A in at most d.* If every vertex of A has at least δ neighbours in B, and no two distinct\n  vertices of A have a common neighbour in B, then δ * #A ≤ #B, since now each\n  u ∈ A lies in at least δ pairs and each v ∈ B in at most one.\n\nBoth are instances of Mathlib's double-counting lemmas\nFinset.card_mul_le_card_mul' and Finset.card_mul_le_card_mul. In their language\nthe neighbours of u in B form B.bipartiteAbove G.Adj u, whose size is\nG.degIn B u by definition, and the neighbours of v in A form\nA.bipartiteBelow G.Adj v.\n\nopen Finset\n\n\nlemma card_le_mul_card_of_adj [DecidableRel G.Adj] {A B : Finset V} {d : ℕ}\n    (hB : ∀ v ∈ B, ∃ u ∈ A, G.Adj u v) (hA : ∀ u ∈ A, G.degIn B u ≤ d) :\n    #B ≤ d * #A := by\n  have := card_mul_le_card_mul' G.Adj (n := 1) (fun v hv => by\n    obtain ⟨u, hu, huv⟩ := hB v hv\n    exact card_pos.2 ⟨u, (mem_bipartiteBelow _).2 ⟨hu, huv⟩⟩) hA\n  grind\n\n\nlemma mul_card_le_card_of_adj [DecidableRel G.Adj] {A B : Finset V} {δ : ℕ}\n    (hA : ∀ u ∈ A, δ ≤ G.degIn B u)\n    (hAB : ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ x ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ B, G.Adj u v → G.Adj x v → u = x) :\n    δ * #A ≤ #B := by\n  have := card_mul_le_card_mul G.Adj hA fun v hv =>\n    card_le_one.2 fun u hu x hx => by\n      rw [mem_bipartiteBelow] at hu hx\n      exact hAB u hu.1 x hx.1 v hv hu.2 hx.2\n  grind\n\n\nTo bound G.degIn B u in terms of the degree of u, we use two lemmas from\nNeighbours In A Set. The Moore bound uses\ndegIn_lt_degree_of_adj_notMem: if some neighbour of u lies outside B then\nG.degIn B u < G.degree u. The cage bounds need degree_le_degIn_add_one, a bound\nin the other direction: if every neighbour of u, except possibly w, lies in B,\nthen G.degree u ≤ G.degIn B u + 1.\n\n#check degIn_lt_degree_of_adj_notMem\n#check degree_le_degIn_add_one\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Moore and Cage Bounds","header":"11.4.2. Counting Between Layers","id":"/Distance/#section-counting-between-layers"}});